(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-ent-anti-infectives-quiz"]={"title":"ENT Anti-infectives & Antifungals Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient on an inhaled steroid has oral thrush and takes several other medicines. Which antifungal is preferred, and why?","choices":["Nystatin, because it is not absorbed","Ketoconazole, because it is the more potent agent","Either one, since neither is absorbed at all","Neither, since thrush resolves without treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The property that limits it to surface infection is the same one that keeps it out of the patient's other drugs. Not being absorbed is also why it cannot interact with the patient's other medicines. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A patient using an inhaled steroid develops oral candidiasis. Which of these situations is NOT given as a setting for it?","choices":["Use of an inhaled steroid","Human immunodeficiency virus infection","Chemotherapy","Recent tympanostomy tube placement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- this is not one of them. The settings given are inhaled steroids, HIV infection and chemotherapy, all of which share suppressed local or general immunity. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A child with acute otitis media finished a course of antibiotics three weeks ago. How does that change the choice?","choices":["Amoxicillin-clavulanate is used instead","The same high-dose amoxicillin is used","A macrolide is substituted","Antibiotics are withheld for a further week"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Recent antibiotic exposure selects for beta-lactamase producers, and that is exactly what the clavulanate is there to cover. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A child has acute otitis media and has had no antibiotics in the last month. What is the mainstay of therapy?","choices":["Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Azithromycin","Ceftriaxone by injection","Amoxicillin, at a high dose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High-dose amoxicillin is first line, and the dose is high for a reason rather than by habit: it is what overcomes the resistant pneumococcus. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which otic ingredient is singled out for causing hypersensitivity?","choices":["Hydrocortisone","Neomycin","Ciprofloxacin","Ofloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A patient whose ear gets worse rather than better on drops may be reacting to the neomycin rather than failing treatment. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What non-antibiotic measure is described as useful in bacterial rhinosinusitis?","choices":["Oral antihistamines","Systemic corticosteroids","Saline irrigation","Topical decongestant for ten days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is the one adjunct named, and it costs nothing in resistance. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is ketoconazole indicated for here?","choices":["Oral candidiasis alone","Fungal otitis externa","Systemic fungal infection","Prophylaxis during antibiotic courses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the contrast with nystatin is the point: one treats infection throughout the body, the other never leaves the mouth and gut. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which cardiac adverse effect is associated with ketoconazole?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Heart block","Cardiomyopathy","QTc prolongation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It matters most alongside its CYP3A4 inhibition, because the drugs whose levels it raises may prolong the QT interval too. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Most acute otitis media involves a virus. What does that fact change?","choices":["It means an antiviral should be given in place of an antibiotic","It means the causative bacterial organisms remain unidentified","It is one reason antibiotics are not automatic in every case","It means the condition resolves without treatment in every case"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A majority of cases are viral, or viral with bacteria alongside, which is the argument against reaching for an antibiotic reflexively. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why is a macrolide a weak choice in acute otitis media even when it is permitted?","choices":["Up to half of pneumococci are resistant to macrolides","Macrolides do not reach the middle ear","Macrolides are contraindicated in children","Macrolides have no activity against Haemophilus influenzae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It remains an option for the penicillin-allergic patient, but the resistance rate is the reason it is not a comfortable one. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A patient with bacterial rhinosinusitis has a penicillin allergy. Which regimens are offered?","choices":["Clindamycin with cefixime","Cefdinir or azithromycin","Clindamycin alone","Nitrofurantoin or trimethoprim"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The first is a pairing rather than a single agent, which is easy to remember wrongly as clindamycin alone. Or levofloxacin. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Beyond the organisms it shares with otitis media, what else causes bacterial rhinosinusitis?","choices":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Candida","Streptococcus pyogenes","Mycoplasma and Chlamydophila","Anaerobes exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The extra names are part of why the starting antibiotic is broader than it is for the ear. Staphylococcus aureus and gram negative bacilli. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What separates viral from bacterial rhinosinusitis, and so decides whether an antibiotic is given?","choices":["The colour of the nasal discharge","Symptoms beyond ten days","The presence of fever at any point","Tenderness over the maxillary sinuses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The timeline does the work, because nothing on examination reliably separates the two. Or worsening after five to six days. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Ciprofloxacin with dexamethasone is effective but expensive. What alternative is suggested?","choices":["Neomycin with polymyxin B and hydrocortisone drops","Oral ciprofloxacin in place of the drops","Ofloxacin with an ophthalmic dexamethasone","Plain hydrocortisone drops without any anti-infective"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An eye preparation used in the ear, which is a deliberate substitution rather than an error, and it reproduces the same two actions for less. An eye preparation used in the ear, which is a deliberate substitution rather than an error. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the stated goal of treating streptococcal pharyngitis?","choices":["To shorten the duration of the sore throat","To prevent acute rheumatic fever and suppurative complications","To prevent transmission within the household","To prevent the infection spreading to the sinuses and middle ear"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Note what is absent: shortening the sore throat is not the reason the antibiotic is given. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which organisms are named as the bacterial causes of acute otitis media?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus, Pseudomonas and Proteus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Group A Streptococcus and Epstein-Barr virus","Candida and Aspergillus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The same three run through otitis media and bacterial sinusitis, which is why the antibiotic reasoning for the two is so similar. Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What adverse effects are described with oral nystatin?","choices":["QTc prolongation and hepatitis","Sedation and dry mouth","Nosebleed and perforation of the septum","Diarrhoea, nausea, stomach pain and vomiting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All gastrointestinal, which is exactly what you would predict for a drug that stays in the gut and is never absorbed. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A child treated for acute otitis media has not improved after three days, and has had no antibiotics in the past three months. What is the next step?","choices":["Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Continue the same agent for a further three days","Move straight to injected ceftriaxone","Stop antibiotics and observe"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Failure at three days is the trigger to step up, and this is the step. Or a third generation cephalosporin. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Nystatin is classified as a nonabsorbable antifungal. Why does that matter?","choices":["It must be given with food to be absorbed","It accumulates in the liver with repeated use","It acts only where it touches, and not elsewhere","It requires renal dose adjustment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Being nonabsorbable is both the safety of the drug and its limitation: it is why it is well tolerated and why it cannot treat anything beyond the mucosa. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"}]};
